title: From drawing to text
check: behaves
mode: vhdl
chapter: Signals and gates
---- intro
So far you have drawn circuits. Engineers usually **write** them instead, in a language called **VHDL**, and a program turns the text into a circuit. On the right, Wire draws whatever you write, as you type.

A VHDL circuit has two parts:

- the **entity** is the outside: the pins. Here, one input `a` and one output `y`. It is written for you.
- the **architecture** is the inside: what happens between the pins. You write that, between `begin` and `end`.

`<=` means "is connected to". The line `y <= a;` is the wire from your very first lesson: `y` follows `a`. Every line ends with a semicolon `;`.
---- task
Connect `y` to `a`: write `y <= a;` where it says `-- write it here`. Then click the switch `a` on the right.
---- start
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity first is
  port(
    a : in  std_logic;
    y : out std_logic
  );
end first;

architecture rtl of first is
begin
    -- write it here
end rtl;
---- answer
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity first is
  port(
    a : in  std_logic;
    y : out std_logic
  );
end first;

architecture rtl of first is
begin
    y <= a;
end rtl;
---- hint1
Replace the line `-- write it here` with `y <= a;`, keeping the semicolon at the end.
---- hint2
The whole line is `    y <= a;` (the spaces in front are only to make it tidy). If the message at the bottom says "syntax error", look for a missing `;`.
---- solution
`y <= a;` connects the output `y` to the input `a`: the same as the wire you drew in the first lesson.
---- done
That one line is a whole circuit. `std_logic` in the entity means "one wire carrying 0 or 1". Everything you drew can be written like this.
